--TEST--
`Core\Log::write` renders a `fields` value JSON has no value for rather than refusing it, and nothing a bag can hold makes a write fail -- `rule:errors/log-write` and `rule:errors/diagnostic-record` and `rule:errors/renderings`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `fields` is `array<string, mixed>`, so a value with no JSON value of its own
// reaches the rendering. `rule:types/bytes`'s `bytes` is the reachable one: it is not
// a string that happens to be binary, it is its own type, and `rule:errors/diagnostic-record`'s
// model carries it as a scalar. The rendering spells what JSON cannot carry as
// a one-key `$`-tagged object -- `nvs_render::json`'s module doc is the home of
// that rule and of why the bytes are hex.
array<mixed> $binary = ["payload" => "text" as bytes];
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Error, "captured", $binary);

// Rendering rather than refusing is `rule:errors/log-write`'s requirement, not a
// convenience: the same helper is what the tier-4 floor calls with its one
// shot, and a reporting path that throws while reporting has nothing left to
// report with. So the record after this one is the next record, never the tail
// of a failed write.
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Info, "after");
--EXPECT--
{"level":"error","msg":"captured","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":9},"fields":{"payload":{"$bytes":"74657874"}}}
{"level":"info","msg":"after","source":{"file":"case.nvs","line":16}}
